<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Aachen</h1><p class="st-meta">1166 CE – 1801 CE (approx.) · capital Aachen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Charlemagne's favourite residence and the coronation city of German kings for six centuries, a free imperial city until the French annexed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> built his palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatine-chapel-aachen">Palatine Chapel</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, around hot springs, and was buried there in 814. Otto I was crowned in the chapel in 936, beginning a tradition of royal coronations. In 1166 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, having had Charlemagne canonised in 1165, granted the town a charter of liberties.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> was governed by a council of patricians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> representatives. Some thirty German kings were crowned there until 1531. Its economy rested on cloth and brass making, as well as pilgrimage to relics shown every seven years. A great fire in 1656 destroyed much of the city, and later spas attracted European visitors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city hosted peace congresses in 1668 and 1748. French revolutionary troops occupied it in 1794, and it was formally annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> in 1801. In 1815 it became Prussian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a> was among the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-aachen.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Aachen" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Laurens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1166 CE – 1801 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aachen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ripuarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aachen, Aix-la-Chapelle</dd></dl></aside></div>

